WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:08.310
This is tungsten, I think.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:14.780
Tungsten is a very hard and heavy element.
It has a number of very important uses. It

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:22.820
is used in the filament of light bulbs, the
ordinary sort of light bulbs because it has

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:29.210
more or less the highest boiling point of
any element and therefore you can use it as

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:36.290
the filament and it can get very hot without
very much evaporation on to the walls. If

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.460
you add iodine in the gas around the bulb
you can run it at even higher temperatures,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.050
the so called quartz halogen lamps and sometimes
you see these in up-market cars.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:58.830
Yes, it’s tungsten. So this is tungsten.
Tungsten is used as an element material to

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:04.489
form the very, very fine wire that we may
have used in old-style electric light bulbs.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:09.070
It is a really quite wonderful metal and it
is actually used in nature. But here we have

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.009
a really quite beautiful sample. This is a
single crystal of tungsten which is mounted.

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:22.229
Tungsten carbide, the compound of tungsten
and carbon is used in the very tip, the balls

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:25.789
in the tip of a biro pen or ball point pen.

00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:31.130
Really quite amazing. I have got some other
tungsten here somewhere and in fact here is

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:36.610
a very fine wire of tungsten and this is precisely
the material which was used in the formation

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.820
of very early electric light bulbs. A really
quite amazing metal.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:49.869
There is also quite a nice book by Oliver
Sacks, who is an Englishman who now lives

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:55.799
in America, called Uncle Tungsten. There are
not many books that have chemical names in

00:01:55.799 --> 00:02:05.969
the title and this is a story of his childhood
and how chemistry really had a big effect

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:06.969
on him.

00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:09.090
That’s lovely.

